course: dictionary
lesson: 25
title: Absorb: taking things in
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To absorb something is to take it in, the way a sponge takes in water. «Never run a microwave empty, because nothing absorbs the energy and it can be damaged.» Here absorb means take in: the energy has nowhere to go, so it is not taken in.
explain: Absorb also works for learning. To absorb information is to take it in and understand it. And to be absorbed in something means you are so interested that you notice nothing else.
explain: Read the words around absorb to find its meaning. «Soft materials such as curtains and carpets absorb sound and reduce echoes.» Swap in "soak up": curtains soak up sound and reduce echoes. It still makes sense, so absorb means take in.
fact: Absorb means to take something in, like a sponge takes in water.
fact: «Dark, dull surfaces absorb heat radiation better than white, shiny ones.»
fact: To be absorbed in something means you are so interested in it that you notice nothing else.
fact: To absorb a shock is to take its force so it does less harm, as a trainer or helmet does.
fact: Plants absorb water through their roots.
exercise: «Soft materials such as curtains and carpets absorb sound and reduce echoes.» What does absorb mean here? | *Take in the sound, so there is less echo | Forget the sound | Make the sound louder | Shout the sound back as an echo
exercise: Why do black cars get hot in summer? | Black paint makes the car heavier | Black cars are always closer to the sun | Dark colours reflect all the heat away | *Dark colours absorb more light and heat than light colours
exercise: Maisie is absorbed in her book and does not hear her name. What does absorbed in mean? | *So interested that she notices nothing else | Soaked wet through | Bored and tired of the book | Stuck inside the book
test: «Plants absorb water through their roots.» What does absorb mean here? | Throw out | Shine on | Share with others | *Take in
test: Which is the best way to test which paper towel absorbs the most water? | Guess which towel looks thickest | Choose the prettiest towel | *Pour the same amount of water on each towel and measure how much soaks in | Use a different amount of water on each towel
exam: «Japan has strict earthquake rules, and many buildings have shock-absorbing bases that let them move without breaking.» What does shock-absorbing mean? | Able to shine brightly in the dark | Able to float on water | *Able to take in the force of a shake so the building does less harm | Able to make earthquakes stronger
